    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Human invariant natural killer T cells (iNKTs) are a set of innate-like lymphocytes that share the properties of both natural killer cells (NKs) and T cells. iNKTs exhibit a variety of different phenotypes and functions, with implications in autoimmunity, transplant tolerance, and the regulation of infections. However, in humans they are rare and not yet characterized adequately. In this thesis, a high-dimensional, data-driven approach was used to provide better clarity between the different iNKT phenotypes and their biomarkers, as well as their major functional population subsets. The data hint at a new combination of conventional and novel markers for these populations. Most saliently, a highly cytotoxic iNKT subset and a pro-inflammatory subset are both identifiable. The identification of these subsets is clinically relevant, as they may correlate with acute graft-versus-host disease (aGVHD) after allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT).",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Smith, Brian and Meyer, Everett and Jones, Patricia and Chien, Yueh-Hsiu. (2019). Characterization of Human Invariant Natural Killer T Cell Subset Heterogeneity via High-Dimensional Analysis. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/zy375gb3263",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "displayLabel": "Preferred Citation",
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
